---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ArrowRight } from '@lucide/astro';

const apps = [
  {
    id: 'showcase',
    name: 'Linkarooie',
    href: 'https://creator-hub.loftwah.com',
    repository: 'https://github.com/loftwah/creator-hub-showcase',
    purpose: 'Mara Quinn’s profile. Four work links and the approved guest contributions she chose to show.',
  },
  {
    id: 'guestbook',
    name: 'Here / there',
    href: 'https://creator-hub-guestbook.loftwah.com',
    repository: 'https://github.com/loftwah/creator-hub-guestbook',
    purpose: 'Where contributions arrive. A guest postcard stays private until Mara approves it, and she can hide it again at any time.',
  },
];

// Read the live release identity rather than repeating a commit from memory. A
// failed read is reported as unverified; it never substitutes an older claim.
// A short module-level memo keeps a warm isolate from making two outbound
// requests on every view without letting a stale answer outlive a release.
const memo: {
  at: number;
  value: Array<{ id: string; revision: string | null; checked: boolean }> | null;
} = { at: 0, value: null };
const memoMs = 60_000;

async function readIdentity() {
  if (memo.value && Date.now() - memo.at < memoMs) return memo.value;
  const read = await Promise.all(
    apps.map(async (app) => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(app.href + '/build.json', {
          headers: { 'User-Agent': 'Mozilla/5.0 (App Composer case study)' },
          signal: AbortSignal.timeout(6000),
        });
        if (!response.ok) return { id: app.id, revision: null, checked: false };
        const body = await response.json();
        return {
          id: app.id,
          revision: typeof body.revision === 'string' ? body.revision : null,
          checked: true,
        };
      } catch {
        return { id: app.id, revision: null, checked: false };
      }
    }),
  );
  memo.value = read;
  memo.at = Date.now();
  return read;
}

const identity = await readIdentity();
const revision = Object.fromEntries(identity.map((entry) => [entry.id, entry]));
const checkedAt = new Date().toLocaleString('en-AU', {
  timeZone: 'Australia/Melbourne',
  dateStyle: 'medium',
  timeStyle: 'short',
});
---
<Base title="Creator Hub: a home for your work, built with your agent" description="A live Creator Hub built through App Composer: two independently owned apps, a real contribution reviewed by its owner, and an exported plan any agent can follow.">
 <div class="hub-intro">
  <h1>A home for your work.<br/>Two apps you own.</h1>
  <p class="lede">Mara Quinn asked for a place for her ceramics, somewhere people could leave a note, and a way to decide what appears. Her agent built it from an App Composer specification. Both apps are live, and each keeps its own source, database and release.</p>
  <div class="actions">
   <a class="button" href="https://creator-hub.loftwah.com">Open the Creator Hub <ArrowRight size={18} aria-hidden="true"/></a>
   <a class="button secondary" href="/init?start=composition">Build your own from this starting point</a>
  </div>
  <p class="small">Mara Quinn is fictional. The apps run in the operator’s Cloudflare account on the operator’s domain; this is a reference build, not a customer deployment.</p>
  <p class="small">Management path used for this reference build: App Composer’s native EmDash panel, with the Creator Hub reference selected. The owner approved, featured and hid the new postcard there. The panel calls each app’s own management API with separate server-side owner credentials; it does not copy the contribution into Composer.</p>
 </div>

 <section class="hub-section" id="brief">
  <h2>The brief, in the person’s own words.</h2>
  <figure class="hub-quote">
   <blockquote>
    “Give me a home for my work on my domain. Let people send contributions that I review before publishing. Let me edit the content, choose what appears, and ask my agent to add or change something later.”
   </blockquote>
  </figure>
  <p>No framework, hosting or database was chosen in that sentence. The specification turned it into two apps, one shared configuration and a set of decisions about who may see what.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hub-section" id="live">
  <h2>What is live now.</h2>
  <div class="hub-apps">
   {apps.map((app) => (
    <article>
     <h3><a href={app.href}>{app.name}</a></h3>
     <p>{app.purpose}</p>
     <p class="small">
      {revision[app.id].checked
        ? <>Serving revision <code>{revision[app.id].revision ?? 'unreadable'}</code> when this page was rendered at {checkedAt} (Australia/Melbourne).</>
        : <>The live release identity could not be read just now. <a href={app.href + '/build.json'}>Check it directly</a> rather than trusting this page.</>}
     </p>
     <p class="small"><a href={app.repository}>Independent source</a> · <a href={app.href + '/build.json'}>Release identity</a></p>
     <picture class="hub-shot">
      <img
        src={app.id === 'showcase' ? '/creator-hub/profile-20261111.webp' : '/creator-hub/guestbook-20261111.webp'}
        width="1440"
        height={app.id === 'showcase' ? 960 : 1500}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt={app.id === 'showcase'
          ? 'The deployed Linkarooie profile for Mara Quinn with four work links and the guest contribution section.'
          : 'The deployed Here / there guestbook showing the approved postcard from Jules.'}
      />
     </picture>
     <p class="small">Deployed screen captured 11 November 2026.</p>
    </article>
   ))}
  </div>
 </section>

 <section class="hub-section" id="journey">
  <h2>What actually happened.</h2>
  <ol class="hub-steps">
   <li><strong>Get Ready.</strong> The agent checked the GitHub and Cloudflare access it already had. Nobody installed anything by hand for this build.</li>
   <li><strong>Describe and configure.</strong> Init exported a versioned specification for Guestbook + Linkarooie: the title, introduction, appearance, four work links and a review-before-publication rule. The form is the same one at <a href="/init">/init</a>.</li>
   <li><strong>Create.</strong> Each app was copied at its pinned commit into its own repository, with its own Worker, D1 database and — for Here / there — its own R2 bucket. Nothing was merged into one project.</li>
   <li><strong>Publish.</strong> Here / there deployed first, because Linkarooie reads approved contributions from it through a service binding. Both answered on their own HTTPS hostnames and reported the revision they were serving.</li>
   <li><strong>Manage.</strong> A guest opened the postcard form, wrote a note and sent it. It stayed private. The owner approved it in the native EmDash panel and featured it in Linkarooie; it became public in both apps.</li>
   <li><strong>Revoke.</strong> The owner hid the same contribution in the panel. It disappeared from both public views immediately, the guestbook item and media returned <code>404</code>, and the card remained unavailable until a later approval.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="small">This record describes the build on 11 November 2026 in the operator’s account. A different person’s build uses their own account, repositories and hostname, and their own owner credentials.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hub-section" id="you">
  <h2>What needed a person.</h2>
  <p>Four actions were genuinely human: signing in to Cloudflare, creating the owner credential for each app, confirming the hostname, and deciding that a contribution could be published. Everything else — repositories, databases, bindings, configuration, deployment and the checks after it — was agent work.</p>
  <p>Your agent gets the same plan in the exported handoff: an ordered list, each step labelled <em>agent</em> or <em>human</em>, so neither of you has to guess who is doing what. Init shows it before you copy anything.</p>
  <p class="small"><a href="/_emdash/admin/plugins/composer-miniapps/apps?site=creator-hub">Open Manage mini-apps with the Creator Hub reference selected</a>. This is the invited-administrator panel and requires a Cloudflare Access sign-in.</p>
  <div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/init?start=composition">See the plan for this composition</a></div>
 </section>

 <section class="hub-section" id="own">
  <h2>What you keep.</h2>
  <ul class="hub-list">
   <li><strong>Source.</strong> Two public repositories with their own history, manifests and release scripts. Either app runs without App Composer.</li>
   <li><strong>Data.</strong> Separate D1 databases and a separate photo bucket. Hiding a contribution is enforced by the app that owns it, not by a copy somewhere else.</li>
   <li><strong>Configuration.</strong> Title, introduction, theme, links and publication rules belong to the apps. A later source update does not overwrite them.</li>
   <li><strong>Export.</strong> Here / there exports its approved items and settings through its own owner API. Linkarooie exports its cards and profile settings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="small">Limits that still apply: code rollback is not data restore, an exported copy of a photo cannot be recalled once downloaded, and both apps here run on the operator’s account and domain rather than a customer’s.</p>
 </section>

 <section class="hub-section" id="next">
  <h2>Try the same starting point.</h2>
  <p>Open Init, choose Guestbook + Linkarooie, and fill in the few details that matter. Copy the handoff to your agent. The specification and the build plan travel together; the agent verifies the environment, builds both apps and reports the addresses it actually reached.</p>
  <div class="actions">
   <a class="button" href="/init?start=composition">Configure a Creator Hub</a>
   <a class="button secondary" href="/ready">Get Ready first</a>
  </div>
 </section>
</Base>

<style>
 .hub-intro{max-width:900px}
 .hub-intro h1{font-size:clamp(2.4rem,5vw,4.2rem);max-width:22ch;margin:0 0 20px}
 .hub-intro .lede{font-size:1.15rem;max-width:68ch}
 .hub-section{padding:36px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-border)}
 .hub-section h2{font-size:2rem;max-width:30ch;margin:0 0 20px}
 .hub-section p,.hub-section li{max-width:72ch}
 .hub-quote{margin:0 0 22px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);max-width:62ch}
 .hub-quote blockquote{margin:0;font-size:1.5rem;line-height:1.45;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:-0.015em}
 .hub-apps{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:32px;margin-top:24px}
 .hub-apps article{border-top:1px solid var(--color-border);padding-top:18px;min-width:0}
 .hub-apps h3{font-size:1.5rem;margin:6px 0 10px}
 .hub-apps p{font-size:15px;color:var(--color-muted);margin:0 0 10px}
 .hub-shot{margin:16px 0 8px;display:block;border:1px solid var(--color-border);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--color-surface)}
 .hub-shot img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}
 .hub-steps{margin:0;padding-left:22px;display:grid;gap:14px}
 .hub-steps li{font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.6}
 .hub-list{margin:0;padding-left:20px;display:grid;gap:10px}
 .hub-list li{font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.6}
 @media(max-width:800px){.hub-apps{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:24px}.hub-section h2{font-size:1.65rem}.hub-quote blockquote{font-size:1.2rem}}
</style>
